<main>

<section id="research">
<div class="wrap">
  <h2>Signal hunting: an intraday seasonality research programme</h2>
  <div class="measure">
    <p>One question ran through this project: <em>can an asset's next move be predicted by blending how it's behaving today with how it has behaved at the same time of day in the past?</em></p>
    <p>I tested that idea across the S&amp;P 500, Bitcoin, WTI crude oil and natural gas, using real 1-minute data from 2017. Each stage changed one thing, measured it honestly, and fed the next. Most stages failed. That is the point: the failures show exactly where an edge can and can't come from, and they led me from price patterns to energy fundamentals.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <div id="c-cross"></div>
      <figcaption>The headline result. Win rate achieved versus the win rate needed to break even, using the same strategy and exit on four markets. Only natural gas cleared its bar.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <!-- Stage 1 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">1</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Copper 1 PM → 2 PM price predictor</h3>
      <span class="tag design">Design flaw found</span><span class="tag">Python · yfinance</span>
      <dl class="kv">
        <dt>Hypothesis</dt>
        <dd>The 2 PM price equals 50% × the average of today's prices before 1 PM, plus 50% × the average 2 PM close over the last 30 days. If the forecast is above the 1 PM price, buy a call.</dd>
        <dt>Why copper</dt>
        <dd>Market research showed copper trading in a defined range, a fair test of a "stable or cyclical" commodity.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>I built a model that pulls 15-minute data, computes both averages, outputs a forecast and a signal, and backtests it. Before running it live, I found a flaw: averaging past price <em>levels</em> breaks whenever price is trending. If copper rose 10% in a month, the 30-day average sits far below today's price, so the model would almost always say "down".</p>
      <pre>pred_2pm = 0.5 * morning_mean + 0.5 * mean(close_2pm, last 30 days)   # level model (flawed)
pred_2pm = p_1pm + 0.5*(morning_mean - p_1pm)
                 + 0.5*mean(close_2pm - close_1pm, last 30 days)        # delta model (fix)</pre>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>What changed:</strong> every later model works in percentage returns, not prices. A return from 40 days ago is comparable to today's; a price is not.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 2 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">2</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Two-dimension model and walk-forward engine</h3>
      <span class="tag design">Engine validated</span><span class="tag">Synthetic self-test</span>
      <dl class="kv">
        <dt>Dimension A, "how today is going"</dt><dd>Today's cumulative return from the open up to the start of the slot.</dd>
        <dt>Dimension B, "this slot's history"</dt><dd>The average return of the same clock-time slot over the previous 40 trading days.</dd>
        <dt>Research basis</dt><dd>Heston, Korajczyk and Sadka (2010) found that a stock's return in a given half-hour predicts its return in the same half-hour on later days, for around 40 days. Gao, Han, Li and Zhou documented intraday momentum.</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre>y[d,s] = P[d,s] / P[d,s-1] - 1                 # the bar's return (what we bet on)
A[d,s] = P[d,s-1] / P[d,open] - 1              # known before the bar starts
B[d,s] = mean(y[d-k, s] for k in 1..40)        # same slot, strictly earlier days
score  = w*sA*z(A) + (1-w)*sB*z(B)             # z = standardised, s = learned sign
trade  = +1 if score &gt; theta, -1 if score &lt; -theta, else 0</pre>
      <p>The weight <em>w</em> is never hand-picked. A <strong>walk-forward</strong> loop fits it on 120 past days, trades the next 20 unseen days, then rolls forward, the way real trading works. I also check whether the chosen weight stays stable from window to window. To prove the engine could find a real edge, I first ran it on synthetic data with a small edge planted in it.</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-synth"></div><figcaption>Synthetic test, 1,560 trades. The planted edge was real (+5.1% before costs), but a cost of just 0.01% per trade turned it into a loss.</figcaption></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Lesson:</strong> an edge about the size of the trading spread vanishes once you trade often. More trades multiply costs, not profit. The best weight was also unstable (standard deviation 0.30 across windows), which signals noise.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 3 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">3</div>
    <div>
      <h3>S&amp;P 500 real-data backtest: what would £100 have made?</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£100 → £83.63</span><span class="tag">HistData · 30-min bars</span>
      <p>I pulled real 2017 S&amp;P 500 minute data from a public GitHub archive. The API was rate-limited, so I fetched the 868 MB repo archive directly, extracted the 222,026-row file, converted time zones to New York and kept regular hours only. The 50/50 weight was fixed in advance, so there was no tuning on the test data. The test ran over 54 days at about 12 bets a day.</p>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Cost per round trip</th><th>£100 became</th><th>Profit / loss</th></tr>
        <tr><td>0 bp (free)</td><td class="n">£95.13</td><td class="n">−£4.87</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 bp</td><td class="n">£89.19</td><td class="n">−£10.81</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 bp (realistic)</td><td class="n">£83.63</td><td class="n">−£16.37</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 bp</td><td class="n">£73.52</td><td class="n">−£26.48</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buy and hold</td><td class="n">£102.52</td><td class="n">+£2.52</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p>The directional hit rate was 44.4%, below a coin flip. On one sample day (15 Aug 2017) the model called 4 of 11 slots correctly.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 4 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">4</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Same-day options and a call + put hedge</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£100 → ~£0</span><span class="tag">Black-Scholes pricing</span>
      <p>Next I tested options instead of the index itself: one option in the predicted direction, and also a call plus a put together as a hedge instead of a stop loss. I priced every option with the Black-Scholes formula at 12% implied volatility, with a 2% bid-ask spread on each side.</p>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Setup</th><th>Average per trade</th><th>Profitable trades</th><th>£100 became</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Single directional option</td><td class="n">−14.5%</td><td class="n">28%</td><td class="n">~£0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call + put hedge</td><td class="n">−8.1%</td><td class="n">18%</td><td class="n">~£0</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Why:</strong> the hedge cost 0.18% of the index, so it needed a 0.18% move in 30 minutes just to break even. The average 30-minute move was 0.06%. It needed 3.2× more movement than the market gave.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 5 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">5</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Weight optimisation sweep</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">Best w = 0.8, still below 50%</span>
      <p>I swept the weight from 0.1 (trust the slot history) to 0.9 (trust today), on both the index and options.</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-wsweep"></div><figcaption>Hit rate for each weight. Every weight scored below a coin flip (42.8% to 47.3%). Results were best at the extremes and worst in the middle, so blending two weak signals just averaged their noise.</figcaption></div>
      <p>The "best" weight, 0.8, turned £100 into £86.35. It didn't win; it lost the least, and it was chosen with hindsight.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 6 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">6</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Geometric mean: trade only when both signals agree</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">Hit rate fell; money rose</span>
      <p>A geometric mean of two signed signals only gives a value when both point the same way. That makes it an agreement filter: both up means buy, both down means sell, and disagreement means no trade.</p>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Method</th><th>Trades</th><th>Hit rate</th><th>£100 became</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Arithmetic, w = 0.5</td><td class="n">644</td><td class="n">44.4%</td><td class="n">£83.63</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arithmetic, w = 0.8</td><td class="n">644</td><td class="n">46.3%</td><td class="n">£86.35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Geometric, all agreements</td><td class="n">281</td><td class="n">43.2%</td><td class="n">£91.73</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Geometric, strong only</td><td class="n">140</td><td class="n">42.0%</td><td class="n">£95.30</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bars it skipped (signals disagree)</td><td class="n">—</td><td class="n">48.8%</td><td class="n">—</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Lesson:</strong> hit rate measures skill; profit measures skill × how often you trade × costs. The filter made money only by trading less. And the bars it threw away had the best hit rate, which is the signature of noise, not signal.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 7 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">7</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Weighted geometric mean</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£1.89 spread across all weights</span>
      <pre>g_w = |A|^w * |B|^(1-w)     # only on bars where A and B agree in sign</pre>
      <p>One mathematical point: when trades are decided by direction alone, this weight does nothing, because the agreement rule fixes the sign. It only matters once a strength cut-off is added, so I kept the strongest 50% of agreement signals for each weight (about 141 trades each).</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-wgm"></div><figcaption>Underlying £100 result for each weight. The best by money (w = 0.4, £96.14) and the best by hit rate (w = 0.7, 44.2%) disagree, and the whole range is under £2.</figcaption></div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 8 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">8</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Bitcoin with a "let winners run" exit</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£100 → £28.18</span><span class="tag">Bitstamp · 525,600 minutes</span>
      <p>So far every test exited after one bar, which makes wins and losses about equal in size. I changed the <em>exit</em>, not the signal: cut losers fast and let winners run, the way trend-followers do, so a below-50% win rate can still pay. I chose Bitcoin because its moves are big enough to clear trading costs.</p>
      <dl class="kv">
        <dt>Signals</dt><dd>A = average hourly return over the last 24 hours (crypto has no daily open). B = average return of this hour over the last 30 days. Trade only when both agree.</dd>
        <dt>Exit</dt><dd>Stop loss at 1× ATR (the average size of recent bars). Sell half at +2× ATR. Move the stop on the rest to breakeven, then trail it 1.5× ATR behind the best price. Checked minute by minute, so the model knows which level was hit first.</dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Trades</th><th>Win rate</th><th>Needed</th><th>Avg win / loss</th><th>Payoff</th><th>Biggest win</th><th>£100 became</th><th>Holding BTC</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="n">909</td><td class="n">36.6%</td><td class="n">38.9%</td><td class="n">+2.76% / −1.76%</td><td class="n">1.57 : 1</td><td class="n">+13.1%</td><td class="n">£28.18</td><td class="n">£1,969.68</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Lesson:</strong> the exit worked. Winners were clearly bigger than losers. The signal still fell 2.3 points short of its break-even win rate, and 379 short trades fought 2017's huge rally.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 9 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">9</div>
    <div>
      <h3>S&amp;P 500 with the same exit</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£100 → £91.90</span>
      <p>352 trades, a 42.3% win rate against 49.6% needed, and a payoff of only 1.02 : 1. The index moves too little in a day, and closing every position by 4 PM capped the winners. Holding the S&amp;P for the year gave £118.85.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 10 -->
  <article class="stage win">
    <div class="num">10</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Cyclical commodities: WTI crude and natural gas</h3>
      <span class="tag win">Natural gas: £100 → £103.94</span><span class="tag">OANDA minute data</span>
      <p>My hypothesis: more volatile, more cyclical assets lower the win rate you need, because bigger moves let the exit build bigger winners.</p>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Asset</th><th>Payoff</th><th>Needed</th><th>Achieved</th><th>Margin</th><th>£100 became</th><th>Buy &amp; hold</th></tr>
        <tr><td>S&amp;P 500</td><td class="n">1.02</td><td class="n">49.6%</td><td class="n">42.3%</td><td class="n">−7.3</td><td class="n">£91.90</td><td class="n">£118.85</td></tr>
        <tr><td>WTI crude</td><td class="n">1.93</td><td class="n">34.1%</td><td class="n">29.7%</td><td class="n">−4.4</td><td class="n">£68.21</td><td class="n">£112.20</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bitcoin</td><td class="n">1.57</td><td class="n">38.9%</td><td class="n">36.6%</td><td class="n">−2.3</td><td class="n">£28.18</td><td class="n">£1,969.68</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td>Natural gas</td><td class="n">1.99</td><td class="n">33.4%</td><td class="n">34.0%</td><td class="n">+0.6</td><td class="n">£103.94</td><td class="n">£83.33</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p>Natural gas was the first profitable result in the programme, and it beat holding gas by about 21 points. As volatility rose, the break-even bar fell from 49.6% to 33.4%, which supports the hypothesis.</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-cost"></div><figcaption>Natural gas sensitivity to trading cost per fill. Realistic gas spreads are 2–5 bp, which erase the profit.</figcaption></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Honest limits:</strong> a +0.6 point margin is within random variation, and the signal itself didn't improve. Volatility and the exit did the work. Volatility doesn't create an edge; it amplifies whichever edge you have, positive or negative.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 11 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">11</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Leveraged natural gas: 2× and 3×</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">Leverage magnifies, never creates</span>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Leverage</th><th>Win rate</th><th>Break-even</th><th>£100 became (no costs)</th><th>Worst drawdown</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1×</td><td class="n">34.0%</td><td class="n">32.1%</td><td class="n">£118</td><td class="n">10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2×</td><td class="n">34.0%</td><td class="n">32.1%</td><td class="n">£133</td><td class="n">19%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3×</td><td class="n">34.0%</td><td class="n">32.1%</td><td class="n">£144</td><td class="n">27%</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p>Win rate and break-even were identical at every level. 3× returned +44%, not +54%, because compounding leverage causes volatility decay. In reality it is worse: leveraged ETFs trade about 6.5 hours a day (most signals fired overnight), they reset daily, and 3× gas products have repeatedly lost over 99% of their value.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 12 -->
  <article class="stage win">
    <div class="num">12</div>
    <div>
      <h3>EIA storage report event study</h3>
      <span class="tag win">56% continuation</span><span class="tag">52 events</span>
      <p>Natural gas is driven by weather, storage and supply, not by its own past prices. So I looked for an edge around a scheduled information event: the US Energy Information Administration's weekly storage report, Thursdays at 10:30 ET. I scanned a year of minute data for the largest recurring price spikes, and the report stood out on its own.</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-eia"></div><figcaption>Average 1-minute price move by time. The Thursday 10:30 report minute averages 0.63%, about 37× a normal minute.</figcaption></div>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr>
        <tr><td>First-5-minute reaction</td><td class="n">0.32% (≈7× a normal 5-minute move)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Correlation of first 5 minutes with the next 3 hours</td><td class="n">+0.15</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Following the first move for 3 hours was right</td><td class="n">56% (58% on bigger reactions)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Approximate 95% confidence interval</td><td class="n">42% to 70%</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this was the first price signal to clear 50%, and it came from real information, not price patterns alone. With only 52 events the interval still includes 50%, so it's a lead to validate across 10+ years, not a proven edge.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 13 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">13</div>
    <div>
      <h3>A fundamental model for natural gas</h3>
      <span class="tag design">In design</span>
      <p>Gas price is supply versus demand, with storage as the buffer. That gives three markers, each scored as a <em>surprise</em> (actual versus what the market expected), because only surprises move price.</p>
      <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Weather (demand):</strong> gas-weighted heating and cooling degree days from the GFS and ECMWF weather models. The change between forecast runs matters most, especially 11–15 days out.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage (balance):</strong> the weekly EIA number against the analyst consensus, and storage against its 5-year average.</li>
        <li><strong>Supply:</strong> US dry gas production, LNG export feedgas, rig counts and winter freeze-offs.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre>Score = w1*z_weather + w2*z_storage + w3*z_supply
z_i   = (actual_i - expected_i) / std(past surprises_i)
weights from: r_next_week ~ w1*z1 + w2*z2 + w3*z3   (regression, validated walk-forward)</pre>
      <p>Most of the work is building reliable "expected" values, especially for weather. I'm now studying the reports themselves before fixing the equation.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Stage 14 -->
  <article class="stage">
    <div class="num">14</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Weekly Thursday-to-Thursday gas options</h3>
      <span class="tag loss">£100 → £0</span>
      <p>I lined weekly options up with the report cycle (buy Thursday 11:00, expire the next Thursday), priced at 55% implied volatility with a 5% spread, using a stop loss and a +100% profit target.</p>
      <div class="chart"><div id="c-weekly"></div><figcaption>Share of winning trades. A wider −50% stop and a tight −10% stop both went to zero.</figcaption></div>
      <div class="lesson"><strong>Why:</strong> a one-week option cost about 3.0% of the gas price, while the average weekly move was 3.8%. A bought option needs the right direction <em>and</em> a big enough move before time decay eats it. A 10% stop was also tighter than the ~9.5% round-trip spread, so the spread alone triggered it.</div>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>
</section>

<section id="method">
<div class="wrap two">
  <div>
    <h2>What the programme taught</h2>
    <ul class="clean measure">
      <li><strong>Being right isn't the same as making money.</strong> Profit = win rate × size of wins versus losses − cost of every trade.</li>
      <li><strong>No way of combining two price signals beat a coin flip.</strong> I tried five: arithmetic, an optimised weight, geometric, weighted geometric, and an agreement filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Volatility lowers the bar.</strong> The exit cut the needed win rate from 50% to 33%, which is how natural gas turned a profit.</li>
      <li><strong>Bought options need direction and size.</strong> They lost in every setup tested.</li>
      <li><strong>Leverage only magnifies.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>The one real lead came from fundamental information,</strong> not price history.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p class="measure">Every model was written in Python and tested on real minute-level data pulled from public GitHub repositories, inside a Linux test environment. Every stage used the same rules: no tuning on the data I'm graded on, costs built in from the start, and results reported even when they hurt.</p>
    <div class="toolkit">
      <span>Python</span><span>pandas</span><span>NumPy</span><span>Black-Scholes pricing</span><span>Walk-forward validation</span><span>Event studies</span><span>Cost sensitivity</span><span>Overfitting checks</span><span>Regression</span><span>Git / GitHub data pipelines</span><span>Bash</span><span>SQL / MySQL</span>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>Show the exit engine (Python)</summary>
<pre>def simulate_exit(i0, d, E, atr):
    stop = E - d*STOP_ATR*atr          # cut losers at 1x ATR
    tp   = E + d*TP_ATR*atr            # book half at 2x ATR
    H, L = Hi[i0+1:end+1], Lo[i0+1:end+1]
    sl  = (L &lt;= stop) if d &gt; 0 else (H &gt;= stop)
    tph = (H &gt;= tp)   if d &gt; 0 else (L &lt;= tp)
    if first(sl) &lt;= first(tph):        # stop hit first: full loss
        return d*(stop-E)/E - costs
    half = 0.5 * d*(tp-E)/E            # target hit: bank half
    run  = running_best(prices after tp)
    trail = max(E, run - TRAIL_ATR*atr) # breakeven, then trail
    return half + 0.5*d*(exit_at(trail)-E)/E - costs</pre>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>
